2.2 Python (verified against resolved environments)

    License metadata is NOT recoverable from the manifests: uv.lock records no
    license field for any entry, and the runtime set has no lockfile. It was
    therefore read from installed package metadata (PEP 566 `License`, PEP 639
    `License-Expression`, and `License ::` classifiers) in two resolved
    environments. Reproduce with:

      # dev toolchain
      uv sync
      .venv/bin/python -c "from importlib.metadata import distributions; \
        print(sorted((d.metadata['Name'], d.metadata.get('License-Expression') \
        or d.metadata.get('License')) for d in distributions()))"

      # runtime set, as actually installed into a container image
      docker build -f agentcore/cost_optimization_agent/Dockerfile -t adi-lic .
      docker run --rm adi-lic python -c "<same snippet>"

    Dev toolchain — 111 distributions, every one licensed, 0 unknown:

      MIT 50 · Apache-2.0 14 · BSD-3-Clause 14 · MIT License 7 · BSD 3
      Apache License 2.0 3 · Apache 2.0 3 · BSD-2-Clause 3 · MPL-2.0 2
      MIT license 2 · Modified BSD License 2 · MIT-0 1 · BSD License 1
      Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause 1 · Apache-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause 1
      Mozilla Public License 2.0 1 · Dual License 1 · PSF-2.0 1
      Apache-2.0 AND CNRI-Python 1

    Runtime set — 142 distributions, every one licensed:

      Apache-2.0 76 · MIT 29 · BSD-3-Clause 13 · MIT License 4 · PSF-2.0 2
      Apache 2.0 2 · BSD 2 · Apache License 2.0 2 · BSD-2-Clause 2
      Apache-2.0 AND MIT 1 · MPL-2.0 1 · MIT-0 1 · MIT-CMU 1
      Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause 1 · Apache-2.0 OR BSD-2-Clause 1
      BSD License 1 · 3-Clause BSD License 1 · Dual License 1 · UNKNOWN 1

    Everything else is permissive. The five entries that are not plainly so,
    resolved individually:

    (d) certifi — MPL-2.0. Weak, FILE-level copyleft, and the only MPL package
        in the runtime set. It ships a CA certificate bundle, is consumed
        unmodified as a declared dependency, and is not vendored, so MPL-2.0
        §3.2 reciprocity attaches to certifi's own files and reaches no project
        code. Present transitively via requests/botocore.
    (e) hypothesis, pathspec — MPL-2.0, both DEVELOPMENT-only (property testing
        and path matching for linters). Neither is imported by any deployed
        artifact.
    (f) python-dateutil — the `License` field reads "Dual License"; the
        classifiers resolve it to BSD-3-Clause OR Apache-2.0, either electable.
        BSD-3-Clause is elected.
    (g) aws-requests-auth — the `License` field literally reads "UNKNOWN", which
        is packaging metadata sloppiness rather than an unlicensed package: its
        classifier is `License :: OSI Approved :: BSD License`. Read as BSD.
    (h) One dev entry is `Apache-2.0 AND CNRI-Python` (the CPython stdlib
        heritage licence) and one runtime entry is `MIT-CMU`. Both permissive.

    No GPL, AGPL, LGPL, SSPL, EPL or CDDL in either environment.

    All Python dependencies resolve from pypi.org. No git URLs, no local paths,
    no editable installs, no private registries.

    Caveat on method: this reads each package's own declared metadata, which is
    sound for a first-pass audit but is metadata, not the LICENSE file text. If
    counsel needs file-level proof, the installed `*.dist-info/LICENSE*` files
    must be diffed against these strings.
